{
  "image_name": "1e5f12ee8fe01dd9deefc19e780bdc93.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares five roll-to-roll solution-processing methods used to deposit functional inks onto a moving flexible substrate. Across all schemes, a web passes over rollers while liquid ink is delivered in different ways to create either continuous films or patterned features.\n\nThree methods—blade coating, slot-die coating, and spray coating—are shown as film-forming approaches. In blade coating and slot-die coating, ink forms a meniscus at the coating head and is spread into a uniform wet layer as the substrate moves forward. Spray coating instead atomizes the ink through a nozzle, apparently assisted by gas flow, producing a mist that lands on the web and forms a coated region.\n\nThe other two methods emphasize patterning. Inkjet printing ejects discrete droplets from nozzles, driven by a piezo element, to place printed lines or features directly where needed. Screen printing pushes ink through a patterned screen with a squeegee, transferring repeated striped or shaped patterns onto the substrate.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights the main distinction between scalable continuous coating methods for blanket films and printing methods for selective patterned deposition on flexible webs.",
